              <strong data-chart-title>Per-student trend</strong>
            </div>
            <small>Gaps mean data is unavailable or the selected program was not operating.</small>
          </figcaption>
          <svg data-trend-chart viewBox="0 0 760 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="trend-title trend-description">
            <title id="trend-title">Education aid per selected student by fiscal year</title>
            <desc id="trend-description">A line chart that updates when funding and student selections change.</desc>
          </svg>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="trend-table-details">
      <summary>See the calculation for every year</summary>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="explorer-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th class="numeric">Selected funding</th><th class="numeric">Selected students</th><th class="numeric">Per student</th><th>Coverage</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody data-trend-table></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell section glossary-section" id="glossary" aria-labelledby="glossary-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="glossary-heading">A plain-language funding dictionary</h2>
      <p>The labels sound interchangeable, but they describe different formulas, payments, taxes, programs, and student counts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-grid">
    <article id="glossary-base-adequacy">
      <span>Formula building block</span>
      <h3>Base adequacy</h3>
      <p>The starting per-pupil amount in the district adequacy formula. It is one component of calculated cost—not, by itself, the final grant paid to a district or the total public investment in a student.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-differential-aid">
      <span>Student-need additions</span>
      <h3>Differential aid</h3>
      <p>Additional formula amounts tied to student characteristics and needs, such as free/reduced-price eligibility, special education, and English-learner status. These additions are embedded in calculated cost in this statewide dataset.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-calculated-cost">
      <span>District formula</span>
      <h3>Calculated adequate-education cost</h3>
      <p>A formula estimate built from base adequacy plus additions tied to student needs, including free/reduced-price eligibility, special education, English learners, and home education. It is not the amount of the state check.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-district-grant">
      <span>District payment</span>
      <h3>Adequacy grant paid</h3>
      <p>The district grant after the formula is applied town by town. In this tool it excludes SWEPT and includes the town-level grant adjustments reflected in the state’s final statewide total.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-swept">
      <span>Property tax</span>
      <h3>SWEPT</h3>
      <p>The Statewide Education Property Tax is assessed at a statewide rate, collected locally, and retained by the municipality. Some presentations count it as state education funding and others exclude it, so the choice must be disclosed.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-charter">
      <span>Program</span>
      <h3>Charter-school aid</h3>
      <p>Direct aid is paid to charter schools. The state files also show charter-related special-education aid paid to responsible public districts. This tool exposes both and counts each only once.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-efa">
      <span>Program</span>
      <h3>Education Freedom Accounts</h3>
      <p>State-funded accounts used for eligible education expenses outside a traditional district-school placement. This tool uses scheduled payments and payment membership from the available state files beginning in FY2022.</p>
    </article>
    <article id="glossary-students">
      <span>Denominator</span>
      <h3>ADM and payment membership</h3>
      <p>District and charter files use average daily membership (ADM); EFA files report payment membership. These funding counts are not the same as a one-day fall headcount, so numerator and denominator must come from matching programs.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <aside class="definition-caution">
    <strong>Why is there no separate differential-aid toggle?</strong>
    <p>Student-need additions are part of calculated cost, while extraordinary-needs and hold-harmless adjustments affect the paid grant. Because labels and columns change across source files, this statewide explorer does not pretend they are one clean, separately comparable field across all twelve years.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="shell section" id="finding" aria-labelledby="finding-heading">
<div class="section-heading">
<p class="section-number">03</p>
<div>
<h2 id="finding-heading">The claimed jump mostly disappears when the method stays the same</h2>
<p>The post says aid rose from $5,251 to $7,100 in one year. That comparison changes what is counted.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="finding-grid">
<article class="finding-card bad-card">
<span>Posted comparison</span>
<strong>$5,251 → $7,100</strong>
<b>+$1,849 · +35.2%</b>
<p>FY2025 and FY2026 appear to use different numerators.</p>
</article>
<article class="finding-card good-card">
<span>Same gross-district method</span>
<strong>$6,870 → $7,096</strong>
<b>+$226 · +3.3%</b>
<p>District grant plus SWEPT, divided by district ADM, in both years.</p>
</article>
<article class="finding-card neutral-card">
<span>Actual state grant, no SWEPT</span>
<strong>$4,518 → $4,707</strong>
<b>+$188 · +4.2%</b>
<p>District adequacy grant paid, excluding the locally raised SWEPT amount.</p>
</article>
</div>
<div class="formula-walkthrough" aria-label="FY2025 and FY2026 calculation walkthrough">
<article>
<span class="formula-year">FY2025 posted: $5,251</span>
<h3>Closest match: calculated cost</h3>
<div class="big-equation">
<b>$817.9m</b>
<i>÷</i>
<b>154,925</b>
<i>=</i>
<strong>$5,279</strong>
</div>
<p>Calculated adequate-education cost ÷ district ADM. The $28 difference could reflect rounding or an earlier estimate.</p>
</article>
<div class="method-switch" aria-hidden="true">
<span>method changes</span>
</div>
<article>
<span class="formula-year">FY2026 posted: $7,100</span>
<h3>Near-exact match: grant plus SWEPT</h3>
<div class="big-equation">
<b>$716.1m + $363.5m</b>
<i>÷</i>
<b>152,140</b>
<i>=</i>
<strong>$7,096</strong>
</div>
<p>Actual district grant plus SWEPT ÷ district ADM. FY2027 reproduces the same way: approximately $7,133.</p>
</article>
</div>
<aside class="plain-callout">
<strong>What is misleading?</strong>
<p>The post compares a cost estimate in one year with a larger, SWEPT-inclusive funding measure in the next year and labels both “state aid per pupil.”</p>
</aside>
</section>
